        "ratings": [
            {
                "comment": "Ossorio should not be teaching torts. She's disorganized and a poor communicator. She cannot bring herself to clearly answer student questions. Lectures are dull, unfocused rambles. She often ends up launching into incoherent tangents. The terrible textbook - written by her friend - only adds to the confusion. AVOID AT ALL COSTS!\n\n\n\n\n",
                "difficulty_rating": 4,
                "quality_rating": 1
            },
            {
                "comment": "She's a lovely kind lady but not great at teaching torts. I learned the most by teaching myself with my classmates at the end of the class. She's good in office hours though so make sure you go to those. Exam was fair based on what we had learned. She cancelled class with no make-up sessions which made it hard for us to get through important units.",
                "difficulty_rating": 3,
                "quality_rating": 2
            },
            {
                "comment": "This Professor did nothing to advance my understanding of the subject matter. She cancelled class multiple times with no option to have a make-up session. She did not organize course material in an efficient way, and  she would often go on tangents that had little to do with course material and seemed more about her opinion. ",
                "difficulty_rating": 3,
                "quality_rating": 1
            },
            {
                "comment": "This professor does not have confidence in the subject matter she is teaching. She does a poor job of presenting materials with clarity, and does not correct or defend student statements to elucidate the substance needed to be derived from the book. It is a great disservice to our University to have her teaching this course.",
